<h1>Orcraft Results and Statuses</h1>
<p>A component reports through its result: a status saying how it went, descriptions saying why, and
  whatever measurements the component chose to declare. This page covers all three, and the two
  mechanisms that set them - <code>checkpoint</code> and <code>handle_errors</code>.</p>

<h2 id="result-fields">Result fields</h2>
<p>Result fields come from the <code>OrcraftResultField</code> namespace and are declared on a result
  class, exactly the way config fields are declared on a config class:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('class MyTestResult(OrcraftTestResult):
	measured_value = OrcraftResultField.Numeric(default=0, label="Measured Value")
	within_limits = OrcraftResultField.Boolean(default=False, label="Within Limits")
	output_file = OrcraftResultField.Text(default="", label="Output File")
	log_orientation = OrcraftResultField.Format(
		default=OrcraftResultField.LogTableOrientation.VERTICAL
	)') }}

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Type</th>
      <th>Value</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>Text</code></td>
      <td><code>str</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>Numeric</code></td>
      <td><code>int</code> or <code>float</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>Boolean</code></td>
      <td><code>bool</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>Format</code></td>
      <td>Presentation-only, used for <code>log_orientation</code>.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>Each accepts <code>default</code>, <code>default_factory</code>, <code>label</code> and
  <code>formatter</code>, with the same meanings they have for
  <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/configuration-fields/#options">config fields</a>. A field with
  a <code>label</code> appears in the logged result table and in the controller; one without stays
  internal.</p>

<p>A <code>formatter</code> changes how a value is displayed without changing what is stored, which is
  how a raw number becomes a percentage or a status becomes a colored word in the log.</p>

<h2 id="inherited-fields">Fields every result has</h2>
<p>Result classes inherit a set of fields the framework maintains itself. You read them, and in a few
  cases write them, but you never declare them.</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Field</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>status</code></td>
      <td>The component's outcome - one of the four values listed under <a class="text-accent" href="#statuses">The four statuses</a>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>failure_description</code></td>
      <td>Why the system under test was judged incorrect. Setting it moves a passing result to <code>FAIL</code> on its own.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>error_description</code></td>
      <td>Why the component could not complete.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>warning_description</code></td>
      <td>Something worth flagging that did not change the status.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>finish_time</code></td>
      <td>When the component finished.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>elapsed_time</code></td>
      <td>How long it took.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>log_orientation</code></td>
      <td>Whether the result table is logged horizontally or vertically.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="statuses">The four statuses</h2>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Status</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>PASS</code></td>
      <td>The component completed and every assertion it made held.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>FAIL</code></td>
      <td>The component completed, but the system under test behaved incorrectly.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>ERROR</code></td>
      <td>The component could not complete - the test itself, or its environment, broke.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>ABORT</code></td>
      <td>The run was stopped before the component finished.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>The distinction between <code>FAIL</code> and <code>ERROR</code> is the one that matters in practice.
  A failure is a finding about the system under test and is worth reporting; an error means the finding
  is not trustworthy, because the test did not get far enough to make it. A suite where half the results
  are errors has an infrastructure problem, not a quality problem, and Orcraft keeps the two countable
  separately so that difference is visible at a glance.</p>

<p>A result starts at <code>PASS</code> and is moved from there - by a failing checkpoint, by an
  assignment to one of the descriptions, or by an uncaught exception, which is what produces
  <code>ERROR</code>.</p>

<h3 id="setting-a-status">Setting a status</h3>
<p>A test rarely assigns <code>status</code> directly. Most of the time it states a finding and lets the
  framework derive the status from it:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('from orcraft import OrcraftStatus

...

def _run_case(self, case: OrcraftTestCase) -> OrcraftTestResult:
	case.result = MyTestResult()

	response = self.query_system(self.config.iterations)

	# ERROR: the case cannot produce a finding at all, so say why and stop.
	if response is None:
		case.result.status = OrcraftStatus.ERROR
		case.result.error_description = "The system under test returned no response."
		return case.result

	case.result.measured_value = response.value

	# FAIL: a failing checkpoint records the finding and carries on.
	self.checkpoint(
		response.value <= self.config.threshold,
		f"Measured {response.value}, within the threshold.",
		f"Measured {response.value}, above the threshold of {self.config.threshold}.",
	)

	# FAIL: assigning a failure description has the same effect as a failed checkpoint.
	if response.code != 0:
		case.result.failure_description = f"The system reported code {response.code}."

	# Still PASS, but worth flagging.
	if response.retries:
		case.result.warning_description = f"Succeeded after {response.retries} retries."

	return case.result') }}

<p>Each status has its own route:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Status</th>
      <th>How a test produces it</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>PASS</code></td>
      <td>The value a result starts at. Nothing to set.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>FAIL</code></td>
      <td>A failing <a class="text-accent" href="#checkpoints"><code>checkpoint</code></a>, or assigning <code>failure_description</code>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>ERROR</code></td>
      <td>An uncaught exception, a checkpoint with <code>is_error=True</code>, or assigning <code>status</code> together with <code>error_description</code>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>ABORT</code></td>
      <td>Produced when a run is stopped before the component finished. Test code does not assign it.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>Note what the example does <em>not</em> do: it never sets <code>OrcraftStatus.FAIL</code> by hand. Going
  through a checkpoint or a description means the reason travels with the status, so the log and the
  controller show why the case failed and not merely that it did. Assigning
  <code>failure_description</code> on a passing result moves it to <code>FAIL</code> on its own, which is
  why the two are never set together.</p>

<p>Only <code>ERROR</code> is worth assigning explicitly, and only when the case has to stop early - it is
  the one status that says the finding is missing rather than negative.</p>

<h3 id="reading-a-status">Reading a status</h3>
<p>Statuses compare as an enum, which is occasionally useful when a caller wants to branch on the outcome
  of a nested component rather than just propagate it:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('flow_result = flow.run()

if flow_result.status is OrcraftStatus.ERROR:
	Logger.log("Skipping verification - the flow could not complete.", color=Logger.Color.YELLOW)
	return case.result') }}

<p>For the usual case - copy the outcome upward and stop if it failed - reach for
  <a class="text-accent" href="#handle-errors"><code>handle_errors</code></a> instead of comparing
  statuses by hand.</p>

<h2 id="checkpoints">Checkpoints</h2>
<p><code>checkpoint</code> is the framework's assertion, available on every component. It takes a
  condition and the message to log for each outcome:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('self.checkpoint(
	measured <= threshold,
	f"Measured {measured}, within the threshold of {threshold}.",
	f"Measured {measured}, above the threshold of {threshold}.",
	details="Measured over the full run, not a single sample.",
)') }}

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Argument</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>result</code></td>
      <td>The condition being asserted.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>pass_msg</code></td>
      <td>Logged when the condition holds.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>fail_msg</code></td>
      <td>Logged when it does not, and used as the failure description.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>details</code></td>
      <td>Optional extra context, logged alongside the outcome.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>is_error</code></td>
      <td>Treat a failed check as <code>ERROR</code> rather than <code>FAIL</code>, abandoning the surrounding scope.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>Both outcomes are logged, so the log records what was checked and not only what broke. This is what
  makes a passing Orcraft run readable as evidence rather than as silence.</p>

<p>A failing checkpoint sets the component to <code>FAIL</code> with the failure message as its
  description, and then <em>continues</em>. That is deliberate: one case can report every problem it
  found instead of only the first. Pass <code>is_error=True</code> when a failed check means the rest of
  the work would be meaningless - a missing file, an application that did not start - and the component
  is marked <code>ERROR</code> and the scope abandoned instead.</p>

<p>Inside a <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/lifecycle-hooks/">setup or teardown hook</a> a failing
  checkpoint is always treated as an error, whether or not <code>is_error</code> was passed. A lifecycle
  assertion that does not hold means the work it was guarding should not proceed.</p>

<h2 id="warnings">Warnings</h2>
<p>A warning records something worth surfacing that does not make the result wrong - a retry that
  succeeded, output that was unusual but valid, a slower run than expected:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('case.result.warning_description = f"Retried {retries} times before succeeding."') }}

<p>The status is unaffected, but the case is counted as a case with warnings, and that count aggregates
  up through the group and suite results. A run can therefore be entirely green and still tell you where
  to look.</p>

<h2 id="handle-errors">Propagating results</h2>
<p>Components do not raise their way up the hierarchy - they return results, and the caller decides.
  <code>handle_errors</code> copies status and descriptions from one result to another and returns
  <code>True</code> when the caller should stop:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('flow_result = flow.run()

if self.handle_errors(flow_result, case.result):
	return case.result') }}

<p>This is the idiom to use after every flow call in a case and after every step call in a
  <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/flows/">flow</a>. It keeps the failure attached to the component
  that produced it while still surfacing it at every level above, so the controller can show which step
  of which flow of which case of which test failed - rather than a single stack trace at the top.</p>

<h2 id="aggregation">Aggregated counters</h2>
<p>Each level above a case counts what happened beneath it, so a summary is available without any code
  keeping score:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Result</th>
      <th>Counters</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>OrcraftTestResult</code></td>
      <td><code>total_cases</code>, <code>passed_cases</code>, <code>failed_cases</code>, <code>error_cases</code>, <code>warning_cases</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>OrcraftTestGroupResult</code></td>
      <td>The same case counters, plus <code>total_tests</code>, <code>passed_tests</code>, <code>failed_tests</code>, <code>error_tests</code>, <code>warning_tests</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>OrcraftTestSuiteResult</code></td>
      <td>The case and test counters, plus <code>total_groups</code>, <code>passed_groups</code>, <code>failed_groups</code>, <code>error_groups</code>, <code>warning_groups</code></td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>Because case counts carry all the way to the top, a suite result answers both questions at once: how
  many of its groups came back clean, and how many individual cases were actually executed to get
  there.</p>
